WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.134 --> 00:00:16.292
Later my mom bought us hot chocolate from the bookstore café.

2
00:00:16.293 --> 00:00:23.327
It was cozy to sit in the café, watching people through the window as they hurried past.

3
00:00:23.328 --> 00:00:26.971
By the time we headed home, it was dusk.

4
00:00:26.972 --> 00:00:31.565
All the lights were coming on in the city.

5
00:00:31.566 --> 00:00:36.774
"You're so quiet, Iggy," said Molly as we walked along.

6
00:00:36.775 --> 00:00:39.225
"I'm thinking," I told her.

7
00:00:39.226 --> 00:00:44.012
I was thinking about what Mrs. Mackey, the author, had said about plots.

8
00:00:44.013 --> 00:00:49.200
I was also thinking about inventing something to help Molly sleep.

9
00:00:49.201 --> 00:00:52.993
"I'm thinking too!" Caswell announced.

10
00:00:52.994 --> 00:00:57.569
"I'm thinking that I want pizza for dinner."

11
00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.854
"We can have pizza," said Lucy.

12
00:01:00.855 --> 00:01:04.296
"I'll put it in the oven when we get home."

13
00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:09.407
"You're a really good cook, Lucy," said Caswell happily.

14
00:01:09.408 --> 00:01:12.690
Molly looked at me and rolled her eyes.

15
00:01:12.691 --> 00:01:18.229
She didn't think a "really good cook" just put a frozen pizza in the oven.

16
00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:24.597
Back in Molly's apartment, Molly and I brainstormed ideas for our story's plot again.

17
00:01:24.598 --> 00:01:30.082
This time we managed to create a long list of possible plots.

18
00:01:30.083 --> 00:01:32.169
"This is good progress, Iggy,"

19
00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:39.555
said Molly, looking at our ideas with satisfaction.

20
00:01:39.556 --> 00:01:43.603
The next day in school we didn't work on our stories.

21
00:01:43.604 --> 00:01:48.026
Instead Mrs. Romano made a sudden announcement.

22
00:01:48.027 --> 00:01:52.828
"Okay, class," she said. "Please open your desks.

23
00:01:52.829 --> 00:01:56.004
It's Desk Inspection time!"

24
00:01:56.005 --> 00:02:07.567
Everyone groaned. Mrs. Romano believes that fourth-graders should keep their desks really neat.

25
00:02:07.568 --> 00:02:11.387
But almost none of my classmates followed that rule.

26
00:02:11.388 --> 00:02:16.323
And now Mrs. Romano frowned as she walked around the room.

27
00:02:16.324 --> 00:02:21.074
"Your desks are very messy!" she said.

28
00:02:21.075 --> 00:02:24.316
"There is no excuse for messiness!"

29
00:02:24.317 --> 00:02:26.477
Jake raised his hand.

30
00:02:26.478 --> 00:02:30.733
"I like my desk messy," he said.

31
00:02:30.734 --> 00:02:36.644
"Me too," Ruby chimed in. "It's cozy that way."

32
00:02:36.645 --> 00:02:42.503
We all nodded, but Mrs. Romano kept frowning and shaking her head.

33
00:02:42.504 --> 00:02:47.753
"There is nothing cozy about a cluttered desk," she said.

34
00:02:47.754 --> 00:02:50.041
"Or a cluttered room.

35
00:02:50.042 --> 00:02:54.175
Both are signs of a cluttered mind."

36
00:02:54.176 --> 00:02:59.485
I wondered what Mrs. Romano would say if she could see inside my mind.

37
00:02:59.486 --> 00:03:04.010
It must be a real mess with all my incredible ideas!

38
00:03:04.011 --> 00:03:11.675
It was at that moment that I got an idea for another invention—one that would help kids everywhere.

39
00:03:11.676 --> 00:03:14.859
I could invent an Amazing Mess-Pusher!

40
00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:19.021
It would clean a room instantly.

41
00:03:19.022 --> 00:03:23.679
No kid anywhere in the world would ever have to clean a room again.

42
00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.124
I could even make a miniature one for cleaning desks!

43
00:03:28.125 --> 00:03:33.493
Later I drew a picture of what my Amazing Mess-Pusher would look like.

44
00:03:33.494 --> 00:03:37.562
Then I showed it to other kids during gym class.

45
00:03:37.563 --> 00:03:43.377
We had to stand in line to practice rope-climbing, so there was lots of time.

46
00:03:43.378 --> 00:03:50.371
"You use the Amazing Mess-Pusher to push all your junk to a corner of the room," I explained.

47
00:03:50.372 --> 00:03:53.896
"It's fast and easy to use."

48
00:03:53.897 --> 00:03:57.035
"I want one of those!" said Evan.

49
00:03:57.036 --> 00:04:00.321
"Me too," said Ruby.

50
00:04:00.322 --> 00:04:02.927
"First I have to make it," I said.

51
00:04:02.928 --> 00:04:06.310
"Then I'll try it and see how it works."

52
00:04:06.311 --> 00:04:11.350
That day after school Molly and I made an Amazing Mess-Pusher.

53
00:04:11.351 --> 00:04:16.035
We used the handle of a broken broom I found in the cupboard.

54
00:04:16.036 --> 00:04:19.863
Next we tied a dustpan to it.

55
00:04:19.864 --> 00:04:23.967
Tate came into the kitchen while we were trying it out.

56
00:04:23.968 --> 00:04:27.598
"What's that thing?" he asked.

57
00:04:27.599 --> 00:04:32.265
"It's an Amazing Mess-Pusher," Molly informed him.

58
00:04:32.266 --> 00:04:35.484
"Iggy invented it."

59
00:04:35.485 --> 00:04:38.347
"Cool," said Tate.

60
00:04:38.348 --> 00:04:40.651
I couldn't believe it.

61
00:04:40.652 --> 00:04:44.487
Tate actually called one of my inventions cool!

62
00:04:44.488 --> 00:04:47.112
But then Tate flashed a grin at me.

63
00:04:47.113 --> 00:04:48.253
"I know, Iggy!

64
00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:57.437
You can use the Amazing Mess-Pusher to push the mess in your room out the window!"
